  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSurface high pressure will build to our southwest today as we\nremain on the western periphery of an H5 s/wv trof axis. Mainly\nsunny skies during the morning will become partly cloudy during\nthis afternoon as some weak daytime instability develops. Other\nthan perhaps an isolated shower/rumble of thunder across SW NH\nit will be a dry day with high temperatures near 70 across the\nnorth with upper 70s to lower 80s south. NW wind gusts up to\n25-30 mph will be possible at times.\n\nThe combination of weakening winds, clear skies, and relatively\nlow dew points will allow for radiational cooling tonight.\nForecast lows range from the middle 40s across some interior\nvalleys with 50s in most other locations. Valley fog is possible\nlate through early Saturday morning. \n\nHigh pressure will allow for continued tranquil weather on\nSaturday with partly to mostly sunny skies and high\ntemperatures ranging from around 70 across the north to near 80\nsouth. Radiational cooling is then likely again Saturday night\nwith low temperatures into the 40s and 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will begin to exit to our east on Sunday as closed\nlow pressure moves across the Great Lakes while spreading a warm\nfront northward across the Delmarva. This area of low pressure\nis then progged to move northeastward Sunday night into Monday\nwith a weak coastal low possibly developing near or over the\nGulf of ME. After a dry and seasonable day on Sunday, rain\nchances will increase Sunday night into at least Monday morning\nwith scattered showers possibly continuing into the afternoon\nhours. Latest ensemble mean QPF shows generally between\n0.50-1.00\" of rainfall during this period but there are some\nindividual members with higher totals. Given the ongoing dry\nconditions across much of the region, this will largely be\nbeneficial rainfall but cannot rule out some minor issues if\nheavy rainfall falls in a short period of time. \n\nMainly dry conditions look to return for Tuesday into Wednesday\nas low pressure departs to our north and east. Temperatures will\nremain near seasonable averages. Showery weather may return late\nweek as another disturbance approaches from the west.",
